  "description": "Resize 2D spatial image matrix to designated dimensions using bilinear four-point coordinate interpolation.\n\n$$\nf(x, y) \\approx [1 - \\Delta x, \\Delta x] \\begin{bmatrix} f(Q_{11}) & f(Q_{12}) \\\\ f(Q_{21}) & f(Q_{22}) \\end{bmatrix} \\begin{bmatrix} 1 - \\Delta y \\\\ \\Delta y \\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nGiven input parameters `image`, `new_h`, `new_w`, return nested 2D list of shape (new_h, new_w) representing resized image.",
  "theory": "## What Is Bilinear Resize?\n\nIn computer vision, spatial tensor operators and geometric bounding-box algorithms extract translation- and scale-aware structure from image grids.\n\n$$\nf(x, y) \\approx [1 - \\Delta x, \\Delta x] \\begin{bmatrix} f(Q_{11}) & f(Q_{12}) \\\\ f(Q_{21}) & f(Q_{22}) \\end{bmatrix} \\begin{bmatrix} 1 - \\Delta y \\\\ \\Delta y \\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n---\n\n## Mathematical Formulation & Theoretical Mechanics\n\nSupervised and unsupervised learning algorithms minimize empirical risk over a hypothesis space $\\mathcal{H}$ while regularizing model capacity to balance bias and variance on unseen test distributions.\n\nThe full mathematical structure is governed by the coupled relations:\n\n$$\n\\text{scale} = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{\\sigma^2 + \\varepsilon}}\n$$\n\n---\n\n## Key Properties & Conceptual Intuition\n\n- **Inductive Bias & Capacity Control:** Regularization penalties ($L_1$, $L_2$, pruning, or ensemble averaging) constrain effective hypothesis complexity and prevent overfitting to training noise.\n- **Decision Boundary Geometry:** Linear models partition feature space with hyperplanes, distance-based methods induce Voronoi tessellations, and tree ensembles form axis-aligned orthotopes.\n- **Related Topics in This Track:** **2D Sinusoidal Positional Embedding**, **Adaptive Average Pool 2D**, **Anchor Box Generation**.\n\n---\n\n## Numerical Stability & Boundary Behavior\n\n- **Conditioning & Degenerate Regimes:** When denominators, norms, or variances approach zero on constant or collinear inputs, additive regularization ($\\varepsilon > 0$) or limiting analytical values prevent division-by-zero instabilities.\n- **Scale & Precision Invariance:** Formulating updates via centered shifts (such as log-sum-exp shifting for exponentials or Welford-style centering for moments) avoids catastrophic cancellation and floating-point overflow.\n\n---\n\n## Computational Complexity\n\n- **Time Complexity:** $\\mathcal{O}(N)$ where $N$ is the total number of input elements, executing in a single vectorized pass.\n- **Space Complexity:** $\\mathcal{O}(N)$ to hold the output array (or $\\mathcal{O}(1)$ auxiliary working memory beyond the result).",
